# Local secrets for `wrangler dev`. Copy to `.dev.vars` and fill in.
#
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
#
# `.dev.vars` is gitignored. Wrangler reads it and puts these on `env` exactly
# the way deployed secrets arrive, so the code path you exercise locally is the
# same one that runs in production — there is no "dev mode" branch anywhere.
#
# These are LOCAL values. Nothing here is uploaded, and `wrangler secret put`
# does not read this file. Production secrets are set separately; see
# docs/deploy-cloudflare.md step 3.

# Required. Seeds the option shuffle. Any 32+ characters will do locally —
# it only has to be unguessable in production, where it protects the
# permutation from being reproduced offline against the public chapter JSON.
CONTENT_PERMUTATION_SECRET=local-development-only-not-a-real-secret-0123456789

# Optional: Google sign-in. All three or none — a half-configured OAuth app
# fails at Google with redirect_uri_mismatch, which is a confusing place to
# debug a missing variable. Leave them blank and the two Google routes 404
# while email/password and guest keep working.
#
# The redirect URI must ALSO be listed on the OAuth client in Google Cloud
# Console, alongside the production one. Google permits http://localhost as an
# explicit exception to its HTTPS rule, which is what makes local testing of
# the real flow possible:
#
#   http://localhost:8787/api/auth/google/callback
#
# Use the SAME client as production, with two redirect URIs. A second client
# means a second secret to rotate and a second consent screen to keep in step.
# Leave all three commented out until you have all three. Filling in only the
# redirect URI is the half-configured state, and the Google routes will say so.
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8787/api/auth/google/callback
